\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Smooth neuron models, and a reset rule}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005} is a smooth map
#{\mathbb{R}^{2}\to\mathbb{R}^{2}}, because #{\tanh} and #{\cosh}
are smooth and the factor #{\cosh((V-V_{3})/(2V_{4}))} is at least
#{1}. The FitzHugh–Nagumo velocity of \ref{fnip-0018} is a polynomial
map on #{\mathbb{R}^{2}}. Both are locally Lipschitz. For each initial
state, \ref{fnip-0029} supplies one trajectory on some open interval
about time #{0}.}

\p{The leaky integrate-and-fire model adds a reset. In the scaled form
##{\dot v=b-v,}
the value #{v} is sent to #{0} when it reaches #{1}.
\citet{(8.1), §8.1.1, p. 268}{izhikevich2007dynamical}
is that rule. The differential equation is linear, and the reset is a
separate instruction at the threshold. The trajectories of
\ref{fnip-0005} and \ref{fnip-0018} are the solutions of those
smooth equations.}
